A number of other results from our group have been published at the same ti=
me as the PRX result, and so </span><strong>I thought now might be a good o=
pportunity to review the current progress of the theory, and do so as candi=
dly as possible, highlighting not only the theory=E2=80=99s successes but a=
lso the challenges it faces</strong><span>. There are also some recent inte=
resting results from the Berkeley and Kyushu University groups which are wo=
rth highlighting.</span></p><p><strong><span>This comes at a time, when eve=
ryone is talking about </span><a href=3D"https://openai.com/index/ten-advan=
ces-in-mathematics/">AI solving math conjectures</a><span>, so I thought th=
is might be a welcome palate-cleanser. Gravity is still hard!</span></stron=
g><span> We=E2=80=99re about to enter a period of rapid advancement in math=
ematics, and that comes with many challenges</span><span data-state=3D"clos=
ed" style=3D"min-width: 0px;"><a data-component-name=3D"FootnoteAnchorToDOM=
" id=3D"footnote-anchor-1" href=3D"https://superposer.substack.com/p/postqu=
antum-gravity-new-results-and#footnote-1" target=3D"_self" class=3D"footnot=
e-anchor">1</a></span><span>, but it=E2=80=99s also pretty exciting when it=
 comes to advancing our understanding of big theoretical questions.  </span=
></p><p>I=E2=80=99ll write the status update over two to three posts. In th=
is post, I=E2=80=99ll just review the recently published results, and then =
in another post I=E2=80=99ll go over the real strengths of the approach, al=
ong with a frank assessment of the weaknesses.</p><p>Our three Physical Rev=
iew papers are:</p><p><a href=3D"https://journals.aps.org/prx/abstract/10.1=
103/2rcd-dzcf">Covariant Path Integrals for Quantum Fields Backreacting on =
Classical Space-Time</a><span> with Zachary Weller-Davies. Phys. Rev. X </s=
pan><strong>16</strong><span>, 031007 (2026); preprint </span><a href=3D"ht=
tps://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07283">arXiv:2302.07283</a><span>.</span></p><p><a=
 href=3D"https://journals.aps.org/prd/abstract/10.1103/7whh-9j22">Emergence=
 of phantom cold dark matter from spacetime diffusion</a><span> with Emanue=
le Panella and Andrew Pontzen. Phys. Rev. D </span><strong>113</strong><spa=
n>, 103521 (2026); preprint </span><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.13=
820">arXiv:2407.13820</a><span>.</span></p><p><a href=3D"https://journals.a=
ps.org/pra/abstract/10.1103/1sy3-dyb6">General form of continuous hybrid cl=
assical-quantum dynamics</a><span> with Carlo Sparaciari, Barbara =C5=A0oda=
 and Zachary Weller-Davies. Phys. Rev. A </span><strong>113</strong><span>,=
 052223 (2026); preprint </span><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.01332=
">arXiv:2203.01332</a><span>, there titled </span><em>The two classes of hy=
brid classical-quantum dynamics</em><span>. </span></p><p><span>And with Mu=
hammad Sajjad, we=E2=80=99ve put out a </span><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/=
abs/2605.05375">preprint</a><span> about the stochastic degrees of freedom =
which arise in the postquantum gravity path integral. This is the paper tha=
t I discussed in </span><strong><a href=3D"https://superposer.substack.com/=
p/teaching-an-ai-to-do-physics-by-wiping">Muhammad vs. the Machine</a><span=
>,</span></strong><span> in which I found that LLMs could reproduce the fir=
st half of this result with a lot of scaffolding, but not the second part o=
f the calculation. That was 5-12 months ago, and a lot has changed since th=
en. We=E2=80=99re now at a point where AI no longer needs the scaffolding, =
although they still get the calculations very wrong sometimes. </span></p><=
