3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"></path></svg></button></div></div></h1><p>=
<span>The covariant path integral paper is probably the most significant de=
velopment, since the path-integral formulation is manifestly covariant, mea=
ning that the formulation doesn=E2=80=99t depend on the coordinates that yo=
u choose. In other words, it respects Einstein=E2=80=99s principle of gener=
al covariance (or more accurately, </span><em>diffeomorphism invariance</em=
><span>).</span></p><p><span>The theory was originally formulated via the H=
amiltonian formulation of general relativity, which introduces a 3+1 split =
of space and time. Although the Hamiltonian formulation of general relativi=
ty is equivalent to Einstein=E2=80=99s manifestly covariant equations, the =
3+1 split makes it harder to see whether the theory is covariant. In </span=
><em><a href=3D"https://doi.org/10.1103/2rcd-dzcf">Covariant Path Integrals=
 for Quantum Fields Backreacting on Classical Space-Time</a></em><span>, wi=
th Zach Weller-Davies, we instead use a path-integral formulation. The acti=
on of the path integral is manifestly covariant. </span></p><p>While it=E2=
=80=99s possible that the fundamental theory of gravity doesn=E2=80=99t sat=
isfy Einstein=E2=80=99s principle of general covariance, that would signal =
a departure from the view that gravity is a theory of spacetime, and I thin=
k this is a principle we should not give up on easily.</p><p><strong>One th=
ing I like about the work is that it brings together a number of different =
types of path integrals, and one can see how similar they are</strong><span=
>. You can check out an </span><a href=3D"https://threadreaderapp.com/threa=
d/1613914993702981632.html">earlier post</a><span>, explaining the differen=
ce between the standard Feynman path integral for quantum systems, the path=
 integral for open quantum systems, the path integral for classical stochas=
tic dynamics, and the combined </span><a href=3D"https://scipost.org/submis=
sions/scipost_202503_00060v1/">classical-quantum path integral</a><span>. <=
